    "evidence_text": "BH1 and BH2 domains of Bcl-2 are required for inhibition of apoptosis and heterodimerization with Bax\n\nBCL-2 was isolated from the t(14;18) chromosomal breakpoint in follicular B-cell lymphoma1\u20133.\n\nBcl-2 has the unique oncogenic role of extending cell survival by inhibiting a variety of apoptotic deaths4\u201313.\n\nThis includes Bax which heterodimerizes with Bcl-2 and when overexpressed counteracts Bcl-214.\n\nWe report here that site-specific mutagenesis of Bcl-2 establishes the two domains as novel dimerization motifs.\n\nRegulation of Bcl-2 expression by HuR in HL60 leukemia cells and A431 carcinoma cells.\n\nOverexpression of the proto-oncogene bcl-2 promotes abnormal cell survival by inhibiting apoptosis.\n\nExpression of bcl-2 is determined, in part, by regulatory mechanisms that control the stability of bcl-2 mRNA.\n\nHuR knockdown also leads to redistribution of bcl-2 mRNA from polysomes to monosomes.\n\nThus, HuR seems to play a positive role in both regulation of bcl-2 mRNA translation and mRNA stability.\n\nSuppression of intestinal neoplasia by deletion of Dnmt3b\n\nAberrant gene silencing accompanied by DNA methylation is associated with neoplastic progression in many tumors that also show global loss of DNA methylation.\n\nNevertheless, we observed a significant decrease in the formation of macroscopic colonic adenomas.\n\nDisruption of Mitf in melanocytes or melanoma triggered profound apoptosis susceptible to rescue by BCL2 overexpression.\n\nClinically, primary human melanoma expression microarrays revealed tight nearest neighbor linkage for MITF and BCL2.\n\nBCL-6 represses genes that function in lymphocyte differentiation, inflammation, and cell cycle control.\n\nBlimp-1, another BCL-6 target, is important for plasmacytic differentiation.\n\nThese data suggest that malignant transformation by BCL-6 involves inhibition of differentiation and enhanced proliferation.\n\nLin28b is sufficient to drive liver cancer and necessary for its maintenance in murine models.\n\nLin28a/b are RNA-binding proteins that influence stem cell maintenance, metabolism, and oncogenesis.\n\nBoth intravenous siRNA against Lin28b and conditional Lin28b deletion reduced tumor burden and prolonged survival.\n\nHowever, as yet, there is no genetic evidence for a role of autophagy genes in tumor suppression.\n\nTherefore, we used a targeted mutant mouse model to test the hypothesis that monoallelic deletion of beclin 1 promotes tumorigenesis.\n\nFurthermore, beclin 1 heterozygous disruption results in increased cellular proliferation and reduced autophagy in vivo.\n\nThus, mutation of beclin 1 or other autophagy genes may contribute to the pathogenesis of human cancers.\n\nThis was compared, using immunohistochemistry, with the pattern of expression of bcl-2, a suppressor of apoptosis.\n\nInstead, apoptosis was attenuated and distributed along the length of the crypt.\n\nMost pertinently, bcl-2 was absent from small intestinal crypt cells at positions 4-5 (the stem cell region).\n\nIndeed, analysis of human carcinomas revealed expression of bcl-2, which in some samples was reciprocal with the expression of p53.\n\nBCL9 promotes tumor progression by conferring enhanced proliferative, metastatic, and angiogenic properties to cancer cells.\n\nTogether, these findings suggest that deregulation of BCL9 is an important contributing factor to tumor progression.\n\nAntiapoptotic BCL-2 is required for maintenance of a model leukemia.\n\nResistance to apoptosis, often achieved by the overexpression of antiapoptotic proteins, is common and perhaps required in the genesis of cancer.\n\nHowever, it remains uncertain whether apoptotic defects are essential for tumor maintenance.\n\nTo test this, we generated mice expressing a conditional BCL-2 gene and constitutive c-myc that develop lymphoblastic leukemia.\n\nEliminating BCL-2 yielded rapid loss of leukemic cells and significantly prolonged survival, formally validating BCL-2 as a rational target for cancer therapy.\n\nLoss of this single molecule resulted in cell death, despite or perhaps attributable to the presence of other oncogenic events.",
